On-disk layout: where runs leave files

Every run writes to known paths only. This file lists each path,
what it holds, who makes it, and who removes it. Read it before
cleaning by hand: deleting the wrong path loses forensics or
collides with a live run, while deleting the right stale path
unblocks the next run. The CLI lives at cmd/main.c.

Layout list

There is one entry per path. Creator means the code that
makes it. Cleaner means what removes it, or manual-only when
nothing does. Statedir means the per-run state directory.
Cgroot means the per-run cgroup root. Rid means run id. Each
entry below names a path, what it holds, who makes it, and
who removes it.

/var/tmp/resonance - default statedir, per-node homes when
rid is 0. Made by sim spawn mkdir 0700 via parent-net and key
setup. Removed by run teardown rm -rf statedir after net
revalid.

/var/tmp/resonance plus rid suffix - per-run statedir for
rid 1..154, suffix keeps runs apart. Made by allocator suffix
plus tun base plus octet. Removed by same rm -rf with
per-run statedir.

statedir/node plus index - per-node home, holds concord or
weld state for node i. Made by spawn mkdir 0700. Removed by
statedir wipe; restart path removes one dir.

statedir/gossip.key - 32 byte shared gossip key, one per
cluster. Made by key setup from urandom plus chmod 600.
Removed by statedir wipe.

statedir/node plus index/concord/config.json - per-node
concord config with id plus memberlist and advertise addrs.
Made by spawn shell printf plus uuid. Removed by statedir
wipe; forensics copies aside first.

statedir/node plus index/concord/journal.jsonl - per-node
append-only journal, concord writes it, harness greps it.
Made by concord binary at runtime. Removed by statedir wipe;
forensics copies to fail dir before wipe.

statedir/node plus index/concord/bbolt.db - per-node kv
store beside journal. Made by concord binary at runtime.
Removed by statedir wipe; wipe-state restart removes journal
plus store.

statedir/node plus index/concord/certs/ - seeded CA certs
ca.crt plus ca.key. Made by spawn copy from repo certs.
Removed by statedir wipe.

statedir/node plus index/concord/memberservice/secret.key -
per-node copy of gossip key, concord needs same key on all
nodes. Made by spawn copy plus chmod 600. Removed by
statedir wipe.

img/rootfs plus probe plus probe-check - local test image
root plus static bins built offline. Made by make img-build
mkdir plus go build. Removed by manual only; make clean
keeps it.

cgroup root/node plus index/daemon where root is the per-run
root - per-node cgroup leaf for daemon plus containers. Made
by cgroup place step mkdir root plus node plus daemon plus
control. Removed by teardown rmdir node plus root, empty
only.

cgroup root/node plus index - per-node parent, stays empty
for containers. Made by same place step. Removed by same
rmdir step.

cgroup root - per-run cgroup root, default path without
suffix. Made by same place step. Removed by same rmdir step.

/var/tmp/resonance-runs.lock - allocator scan mutex with
flock. Made by acquire open create 0600 plus flock exclusive.
No clean needed; held only across scans.

/var/tmp/resonance-run-N.lock - one lock file per run id,
1..154. Made by acquire open create 0600 plus flock exclusive
nonblocking on the first free id. Held open till the run
process exits, so ids recycle on exit and crash alike with
no manual reset. All busy fails loudly.

/var/tmp/resonance-rsuite-XXXXXX - per-run rsuite log dir
from mkdtemp, one per bin/rsuite run call. Made by log dir
maker via mkdtemp. Persists by design, manual rm -rf; none
removes it automatically.

run dir/base.log plus base-N.log - per-file logs, base is
file basename, clash suffix on clash up to 100 tries. Made
by log open exclusive plus join. Persists by same rule; none
removes them automatically.

statedir-fail/suite plus seconds - forensics aside dir
beside statedir, keeps logs plus journals past wipe. Made by
forensics mkdir plus copy. Removed by manual only; statedir
wipe does not cover it.

/tmp/resonance-img plus pid plus name for hdrs, body, hdump,
code - per-process curl scratch. Made by temp path with pid.
Removed by manual rm -f; no auto unlink in img path.

/tmp per-push layer plus config plus manifest - per-push
image layer plus config plus manifest. Made by push snprintf
plus tar plus writes. Removed by manual rm -f.

statedir/node number log - per-node concord log saved from
pipe chunks. Made by poll log append. Removed by forensics
tails then wipe.

statedir/weld number log - per-node weld log, same role on
weld runs. Made by weld log save append. Removed by same
tail then wipe.

statedir/ros number log - per-node ROS capture where a peer
runs. Made by weld ROS peer start capture in per-run
statedir. Removed by same tail then wipe.

Per-node versus per-file logs

Two log sets serve two readers. Per-node logs live under the run
statedir and serve the verdict: peers grep, marker grep, observed
checks, and tail forensics read them. Names split by SUT: node logs
on concord, weld logs plus ros logs where a peer runs on weld.
Per-file logs live under the rsuite run dir and serve the operator:
one file per YAML input, named for the input basename with a numeric
suffix on clash. They hold the child stdout plus stderr bytes plus
suite phase and frac lines. Statedir wipes delete per- node logs
after forensics. Nothing deletes per-file logs. If you look for a
verdict in the per-file log or for operator context in a wiped
statedir, you will look in the wrong place.

Caps and charset

Fixed buffers cap names. Long names fail instead of truncating and
touching the wrong path. If a run refuses a long statedir or odd
character, shorten and sanitize rather than forcing it: the refusal
guards rm -rf and device paths.

statedir holds 128 bytes and cgroot holds 128 bytes. Suite copies
use dir length 128 and path length 192. statedir, ns prefix, tun
prefix, and cgroot allow only letters, digits, plus slash,
underscore, dot, hyphen; all reach system so other bytes refuse.
Every tun name must fit the interface name cap minus one and base
must not be negative. Underlay must be a sane unicast /24 clear of
loopback, multicast, and the 10.0.0.0/16 overlay. Truncated rm -rf
and rmdir commands refuse to run.

Run id mapping

Small ints map to disjoint names, addrs, and devices. The allocator
hands them out. Use the mapping to tell runs apart when two runs
share a host. If you reuse an id by hand you will alias TUN names or
octets and the runs will fight.

Range is 1..154 from the allocator. Run 0 is the historic default
with no suffix. For rid above 0: statedir gains a rid suffix, ns
prefix gains the rid, tun base is rid times the per run map max,
third octet is 100 plus rid with base 100, cgroot gains a rid
suffix. Max 100 plus 154 is 254 so the octet stays valid, and the
worst tun index stays five digits. Ids are monotonic with no reuse;
wipe the counter file to reset after runs end. Missing or empty
counter reads as 0; over-long or garbage rejects as corrupt; I/O
error fails instead of handing id 1; max long cannot overflow by
range check before bump; publish is temp plus rename at 0600 with
tmp fsync plus best-effort fsync of the parent dir; full at 154
prints run ids exhausted and fails.

Host versus isolated footprint

Old runs touch the host. New runs touch nothing on the host except
the allocator file. Run isolated or serial: two host runs cannot run
together because both claim the host route and both bind the same
memberlist port. If the second of two parallel runs flaps, check
this first.

Host path keeps node 0 in the host netns for check parity and
single-run debug. Isolated path puts each node in its own netns with
every TUN inside its netns, no host tun, no host addr, no host
multicast route. Memberlist binds all interfaces port 7946 with
advertise and anchor addrs at under octet port 7946. Isolated runs
with distinct nets share only the allocator file. Executor always
selects isolated. Pairing is strict: host setup needs isolate flag 0
and isolated needs nonzero, else a loud run failure with no side
effects.

Dev-capable statedir

State must live where devices work. STATEDIR must sit on a
dev-capable filesystem because container device nodes are made in
bundles below it, and a nodev mount such as /tmp tmpfs refuses every
device open. Put statedir on /var/tmp, not /tmp, or every spawn
fails. This is a documented header requirement; no runtime probe for
it was found.

Cgroup leaves

Cleanup removes only empty dirs. Teardown runs rmdir on each node
dir then on the root itself with stderr nulled. Rmdir removes empty
dirs only, so strays fail silently there by design. Truncated names
never run and still return true. Do not use rm -rf on cgroupfs:
rmdir is the guard.

